body:
I am currently dealing with some large, and very sparse data structures, and have the use case of creating a single CompositeByteBuf (A) with a number of other CompositeByteBuf (B-C), mixed with some Slices of other CompositeByteBuf (D-F), mixed with UnpooledHeapByteBuf, etc...

Once I've constructed a composite based on slices of another composite (which is based on composites, heap byte bufs, slices, etc), and attempt to get nio ByteBuffer[] via the nioBuffers() method, I get a UnsupportedOperationException .

The following code illustrates the issue.

``` java
import static io.netty.buffer.Unpooled.buffer;
import static io.netty.buffer.Unpooled.compositeBuffer;
import static org.junit.Assert.assertTrue;
import io.netty.buffer.ByteBuf;
import io.netty.buffer.CompositeByteBuf;
import io.netty.buffer.Unpooled;

import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

import org.junit.Before;
import org.junit.Test;

public class TestNetty {

class Section {
    public int offset;
    public int length;
    public Section(int off, int len){
        offset = off;
        length = len;
    }
}

@Test
public void testSectionsOverlap3() {
    // buf, buf2, buf3, buf4, buf5, buf6, buf7 are a mix of other CompositeByteBuf 
    // or UnpooledHeapByteBuf with data read from disk/network, but for test are
    // just plain empty buffers
    ByteBuf buf = buffer(32 * 1024);
    buf.writerIndex(64* 1024);
    ByteBuf buf2 = buffer(64* 1024);
    buf2.writerIndex(32 * 1024);
    ByteBuf buf3 = buffer(32 * 1024);
    buf3.writerIndex(32 * 1024);
    ByteBuf buf4 = buffer(32 * 1024);
    buf4.writerIndex(32 * 1024);
    ByteBuf buf5 = buffer(64* 1024);
    buf5.writerIndex(64* 1024);
    ByteBuf buf6 = buffer(32 * 1024);
    buf6.writerIndex(32 * 1024);
    ByteBuf buf7 = buffer(128* 1024);
    buf7.writerIndex(128* 1024);

    // Create a slice of a composite
    CompositeByteBuf cb2 = compositeBuffer();
    cb2.addComponents(buf4, buf5, buf6);
    ByteBuf slice = cb2.slice(12*1024, 96*1024);

    // Create a composite, based on buffers and a slice
    CompositeByteBuf cb = compositeBuffer();
    cb.addComponents(buf, buf2, buf3, slice, buf7);

    // Create a new composite based on slices of composite cb.
    cb.writerIndex(352*1024); 

    List<Section> fragments = new ArrayList<>();
    fragments.add(new Section(0, 10 * 1024));
    fragments.add(new Section(11 * 1024, 10 * 1024));
    fragments.add(new Section(23 * 1024, 10 * 1024));
    fragments.add(new Section(34 * 1024, 10 * 1024));
    fragments.add(new Section(44 * 1024, 28));
    fragments.add(new Section(45 * 1024, 32 * 1024));
    fragments.add(new Section(95 * 1024, 8 * 1024));
    fragments.add(new Section(117 * 1024, 9 * 1024));
    fragments.add(new Section(127 * 1024, 40 * 1024));
    fragments.add(new Section(200 * 1024, 20 * 1024));
    ByteBuf composite = compose(cb, fragments);

    ByteBuffer[] bufs = composite.nioBuffers(); // get a UnsupportedOperationException(); here
    for(ByteBuffer b : bufs){
        // ... read b and operate as needed.
    }
}

public static ByteBuf compose(ByteBuf bb, List<Section> fragments) {
    final List<ByteBuf> components = new ArrayList<ByteBuf>();
    for (Section s : fragments) {
        components.add(bb.slice(s.offset, s.length));
    }
    return Unpooled.wrappedBuffer(components.toArray(new ByteBuf[components.size()]));
}

}
```

Looking through the source, DefaultCompositeByteBuf.nioBuffer(index, length) can only handle composites with a single component? (SliceByteBuf.nioBuffer() calls buffer.nioBuffer on the underlying buffer before checking if it's a composite or not)? 

Is this correct, or a bug in either SliceByteBuf or DefaultCompositeByteBuf nioBuffer() methods or is this still on the TODO list for the 4.0 release?

PS. Using current master branch from GitHub (4.0.0-CR2-SNAPSHOT)
